.TH "APRS" "1" "October 23, 2026" "ka9q-radio" "ka9q-radio Manual"
.SH NAME
aprs \- derive antenna pointing from received APRS positions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aprs
.RI "[-L latitude] [-M longitude] [-A meters] [-s callsign] [-I destination] [-R rotor] [-v] [-V]"
.SH STATUS
.B Experimental, incomplete
.SH DESCRIPTION
aprs receives decoded AX.25 APRS position packets, computes azimuth,
elevation, and range relative to a configured observing location, and writes
decoded information to standard output. It also connects to a rotor-control
service and sends set-position commands. It is not an APRS-IS feeder.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B "\-L degrees"
Set observer latitude in decimal degrees; north positive. Default 32.8604.
.TP
.B "\-M degrees"
Set observer longitude in decimal degrees; east positive. Default -117.1889.
.TP
.B "\-A meters"
Set observer altitude in meters. Default zero.
.TP
.B "\-s callsign"
Process the selected APRS source callsign; default processes all stations.
.TP
.B "\-I destination"
Set AX.25 RTP input destination. Default ax25.local:5004.
.TP
.B "\-R destination"
Set rotor-control destination, intended host[:port]. The implementation
defaults to 127.0.0.1:4533. See current limitation below.
.TP
.B "\-v"
Increase diagnostics.
.TP
.B "\-V"
Print version and exit.
.SH LIMITATIONS
This is experimental rotor-control code. Set the observer position explicitly
and select a source before using it with a physical rotator. The current rotor
destination parser builds the resolver hostname before splitting a :port
suffix; its host:port handling needs correction or verification. It attempts
to connect to the rotor service even when only a textual display is desired.
.SH SEE ALSO
packetd(8), aprsfeed(8), rotctld(1)
.PP
Project documentation: https://github.com/ka9q/ka9q-radio
